WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:13.080
The Hydra of mythology was a massive monster.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:19.720
If you cut off one of its heads, two more
would grow back in its place, and its teeth

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:23.670
had the power to raise skeletons.

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:27.290
It lived in the lake of Lerna in the Argolis.

00:00:27.290 --> 00:00:30.840
We found our Hydra at a shopping mall.

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:42.160
See, hydras are microanimals found in freshwater
around the world, often attached to duckweed

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:46.590
and wood floating on unpolluted ponds and
streams.

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:52.750
And while James, our enterprising master of
microscopes, does often collect his various

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:59.570
microbial samples from the great outdoors,
in Poland where James lives, the ponds and

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:03.590
rivers are too cold most of the year for hydras.

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:11.040
But he’d heard that hydras are a problem
for aquarium owners, thus a trip to the mall.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:17.439
This is the first hydra he found.

00:01:17.439 --> 00:01:25.350
The microcosmos is a many splendored thing,
and sometimes what it uncovers is a toxin-tentacled,

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:34.690
potentially immortal hunter hitching a ride
on an aquatic snail inside a shopping mall aquarium.

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:40.160
The good news is, it doesn’t have any teeth
so I think we’re safe from the skeletons.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:49.780
Hydra belong to the phylum Cnidaria, which
makes them a relative of jellyfish and sea anemone.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:57.749
Their bodies are small, about 10-20 millimeters
in length, and also quite simple, composed

00:01:57.749 --> 00:02:04.490
of two cellular layers around a central cavity,
which acts sort of like the hydra’s stomach.

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:10.819
At the end of this cavity, the hydra’s body
gives way to tentacles, which surround the

00:02:10.819 --> 00:02:12.610
hydra’s mouth.

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:20.380
Like our snail-bound friend from earlier,
hydras usually secure themselves to hard surfaces,

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:23.180
including stones and bits of plants.

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:30.260
There, the hydra waits for food, its tentacles
stretching up to three times the length of

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:38.830
its body in search for prey like crustaceans,
insects, and even small fish.

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:44.560
Much of the hydra’s hunting ability lies
in those tentacles, which are armed with little

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:49.750
projectile cellular capsules called nematocysts.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:57.440
Here, you can see some nematocysts that were
discharged by a hydra, which happened due

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:03.040
to mechanical stimulation of the nematocysts
as the sample was being prepared.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:08.800
The cylindrical body of the nematocyst is
attached to a long thread, which can serve

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:10.940
different functions.

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:17.150
One type of nematocyst is equipped with a
thread that is designed to wind around the

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:22.730
hydra’s prey, while the thread of another
kind of nematocyst has an opening at the tip,

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:31.500
which allows it to puncture the surface of
the hydra’s prey to inject a neurotoxin.

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:36.870
When prey brushes up against the hydra’s
tentacles, the nematocysts are discharged

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:41.400
quickly, and by quickly, I mean very fast.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:53.440
They experience around 5,410,000 g of force
in as little a time as 700 ns.

00:03:53.440 --> 00:04:00.280
That incredible speed helps the thread of
the nematocyst act like a harpoon and catch

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:01.740
its prey.

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:09.340
When the hydra isn’t feeding, its mouth
is sealed with the rest of its body in a continuous

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:10.810
sheet of cells.

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:21.400
So to eat, the hydra essentially has to tear
a hole open in its body to let the prey in,

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:28.720
which it does by sending neuronal signals
that stretch the mouth apart until finally,

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:31.150
it rips open.

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:37.960
Even more remarkable, hydras can open their
mouths wider than their body, which lets them

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:42.960
eat prey that is bigger than them.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:48.300
Inside the hydra, the prey is broken down
into particles that are taken up by surrounding

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:50.090
digestive cells.

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:56.240
Now hydra, let’s just get this out of the
way, don’t have an anus, so the remaining

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:03.800
undigested bits of food go out the same way
they came in: through the mouth.

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:10.380
You may have noticed that the hydras we’ve
shown are either brown or green.

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:16.220
You may have guessed, based on occurrences
of the color green in previous episodes, that

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:19.290
it suggests something about chloroplasts.

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:26.870
Indeed, green hydra—which are their own
distinct group called Chlorohydra—are occupied

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:34.780
by unicellular algae, which take up residence
inside of vacuoles within the hydra’s cells,

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:41.790
performing photosynthesis and providing sugars
in exchange for other nutrients and protection

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:43.980
from the hydra.

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:52.480
Hydras have fascinated biologists for centuries
thanks to their spectacular regeneration abilities.

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:58.960
They can withstand all manner of violence:
cut a hydra in two, and you’ll end up with

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:00.370
two hydras.

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:04.940
Cut it into twenty pieces, and you’ll have
twenty hydras.

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:09.540
Turn the hydra inside-out, and it will recover.

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:15.740
Scientists have even blended hydras down to
their cellular components, spun them in a

00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:25.180
centrifuge so they pack together, and watched
as the cells sort themselves back into hydra.

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:32.020
For the organism, this all boils down to a
unique ability to continuously renew their

00:06:32.030 --> 00:06:40.389
body through stem cells, which is why hydra
are sometimes called the eternal embryo.

00:06:40.389 --> 00:06:46.990
This is not to say that they cannot die—if
food runs low or water quality becomes poor

00:06:46.990 --> 00:06:52.150
or something eats them, then yes, the hydra
too will succumb.

00:06:52.150 --> 00:07:00.120
But scientists studying hydra over several
years have found no sign of aging in the organisms,

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:07.240
which means that barring all of those challenges
we mentioned earlier, hydras may be immortal.

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:15.200
But as mortal beings ourselves, the possibility
of hydra’s immortality will likely remain

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:21.210
just that to us—an unconfirmed likelihood.

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:29.430
The simplicity of the hydra’s body belies
the complexity of its life, from algal symbionts,

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:36.300
to its periodically torn-open mouth, to its
endless renewal and regeneration.

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:43.160
All this, from an organism we found in a shopping
mall aquarium.

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:50.630
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:54.760
If you want to see more from our Master of Microscopes, James, check out

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:57.919
Jam and Germs on Instagram.

